The DaVinci Configurator command line interface has six different use cases: 

Use case: Code generation
    Execute the code generation for a given DaVinci project.

    Usage example:
    DVCfgCmd --project Project.dpa --generate
    DVCfgCmd --project Project.ecuc.arxml --generate --modulesToGenerate "/MICROSAR/Det"
    
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option                     | Arguments    | Mandat. | Description                                       |
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -p, --project               <PROJECT_FILE>  yes       Specifies the absolute path to the DPA project file or the ECUC file.
    -g, --generate                              or -v     Generate the given project specified in <PROJECT_FILE>.
                                                            Unless otherwise stated all modules are generated.
                                                            The -g option also executes --validate
    -v, --validate                              or -g     Validate the given project specified in <PROJECT_FILE>.
    -m, --modulesToGenerate     <MODULES>       no        Specifies the module definition references, which should be generated by the -g switch. 
                                                            If empty (""), then no internal generators are executed.
                                                            Separate multiple modules by a ','. 
                                                              Syntax: --modulesToGenerate "<identifier>,<identifier> ..."
                                                            The identifier of a module is the AUTOSAR Definition path.
                                                              E.g. /MICROSAR/Nm, /MICROSAR/CanIf
                                                                --modulesToGenerate "/MICROSAR/Nm,/MICROSAR/CanIf"
                                                             You could also pass the shortname of the module, like "Rte", but be aware,
                                                             if multiple modules have the same shortname, all modules will be generated!   
                                                             With argument 'default' all modules stored in .dpa file will be generated.   
    -x, --modulesToExclude      <MODULES>       no        Specifies the module definition references, which should not be generated by the -g switch. 
                                                            Separate multiple modules by a ','. 
                                                              Syntax: --modulesToExclude "<identifier>,<identifier> ..."
                                                            The identifier of a module is the AUTOSAR Definition path.
                                                              E.g. /MICROSAR/Nm, /MICROSAR/CanIf
                                                                --modulesToExclude "/MICROSAR/Nm,/MICROSAR/CanIf"
                                                             You could also pass the shortname of the module, like "Rte", but be aware,
                                                             if multiple modules have the same shortname, all modules will be excluded!   
    --extGenStepsToGenerate     <GEN_STEPS>     no        Specifies the External Generation Steps, which should be generated by the -g switch. 
                                                            If empty (""), then no External Generation Steps are executed.
                                                              Syntax: --extGenStepsToGenerate "<identifier>,<identifier> ..."
                                                            The identifier of a step is the step name. Separate multiple steps by a ','. 
                                                              E.g. MyStep,DioExtStep,SwcGenStep   
                                                                --extGenStepsToGenerate "DioExtStep,SwcGenStep"				
                                                            With argument 'default' all extGenSteps stored in .dpa file and SIP will be generated (The genTyp is considered)       
    --swcsToGenerate            <SWCs>          no        Specifies the applications software components (SWC) a template should be generated for.
                                                            If empty (""), then no templates are generated
                                                            Separate multiple SWCs by a ','.
                                                              Syntax: --swcsToGenerate "<identifier>, <identifier>..."
                                                            The identifier is the ShortName (or qualified AutosarPath) of each SwComponentType used within the ECU composition
                                                              E.g. SWC_A, SWC_B, /ComponentTypes/SWC_C
                                                                --swcsToGenerate "SWC_A, SWC_B, /ComponentTypes/SWC_C"
                                                            With argument 'default' a template  will be generated for all software components activated in .dpa file.   
                                                            If no arguments are passed to this option, all available SwComponentTypes are generated.
                                                         									
    --genArg                    <GEN_ARGS>      no        Passes arguments to the specified code generators. 
                                                             Syntax: --genArg "<DEFINTION>:<Argument>" or --genArg="<DEFINTION>:<Argument>".
                                                             The Definition is the AUTOSAR Definition path of the module to generate.
                                                                E.g. /MICROSAR/Nm, /MICROSAR/CanIf
                                                             You could also pass the shortname of the module, like "Rte", but be aware,
                                                             if multiple modules have the same shortname, all modules will get the argument!   
                                                             You can pass multiple --genArg parameters.
                                                               E.g. --genArg /MICROSAR/Nm:DoSomethingSpecial=true 
                                                                    --genArg="Rte:DoAnotherThing=true"
                                                             Multiple arguments for one definition can be passed in one --genArg argument or in several.
                                                             If several --genArgs are used for the same definition, the values are concatenated with a blank as separator.
                                                               E.g. --genArg /MICROSAR/Nm:Arg1=true Arg2=false
                                                               or   --genArg /MICROSAR/Nm:Arg1=true --genArg /MICROSAR/Nm:Arg2=false
   --saveProject                                no        Saves the project after validation or generation
                                                             Generators may modify the project in the calculation phase before the actual generation has started.
                                                             This option will persist these modifications.

   --syncSystemDescription                      no        Performs the synchronization of the System Description after loading the project.
                                                             This might modify the project, so please consider also the usage of the --saveProject option. 
                                                             
   --genType                    <GEN_TYPE>      no        Specifies the generation process type ("REAL" => Real Target or "VTT" => vVIRTUALtarget)
                                                             Syntax: --genType <GEN_TYPE>
                                                               E.g. --genType REAL
                                                             If no --genType is set, the default depends on the <TargetType>-setting from dpa file.
                                                             
   --genDataDir                 <GEN_DATA_DIR>  no        Specifies the output directory for the generated data files. 
                                                             This option is only valid if no dpa file is used as project file.
                                                             Syntax: --genDataDir <GEN_DATA_DIR>
                                                               E.g. --genType D:\gen\output
                                                             If no --genDataDir is set, the default output directory is used for the generated data files.
                                    
   --keepGenTempFiles                           no        Keeps the temporary files created during generation after the generation process is finished.
   
   --buildVTTProject                            no        Enables building of the VTT project.   
  
   --reportFile                 <REPORT_FILE>   no        Specifies the path to the DaVinciExecutionReport file to export the execution results to a XML file. 
                                                            The schema of the XML file is located in '<DAVINCI_CFG_DIR>/Schemas/DaVinciExecutionReport.xsd'
                                                               Syntax: --reportFile <REPORT_FILE>
                                                                 E.g. --reportFile "D:\reports\DaVinciExecutionReport.xml"
                                                            Additionally a html file is created in same location to show the results in a browser. 
                                                            This option can be disabled with a the second argument value ';noHtml'.
                                                            	 Syntax: --reportFile <REPORT_FILE>|noHtml
                                                                 E.g. --reportFile "D:\reports\DaVinciExecutionReport.xml";noHtml
                                                                                                                                      
     At least one of the options -g or -v must be specified.     




Usecase DaVinci Configurator Automation Script execution:
    The DaVinci Configurator executes user defined scripts.
    Usage examples:
    DVCfgCmd --scriptTask ApplTask
    DVCfgCmd --scriptTask ProcessProject --project Project.dpa
    DVCfgCmd --scriptTask UpdateProjectTask --taskArgs "--updateInputFolder ""d:\InputDataFolder"" --diagOnly"
  
    
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option                     | Arguments    | Mandat. | Description                                       |
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -p, --project               <PROJECT_FILE>  no        Specifies the absolute path to the DPA project file or the ECUC file.
    -s, --scriptTask            <TASK_NAME>     no        Specifies the script task to execute. 
                                                             Separate multiple tasks to execute by a ','.
                                                               Syntax: --scriptTask "TaskName1,TaskName2 ..."
                                                             The <TASK_NAME> could also be full qualified with the script name prefix, to call a task with clashing names.
                                                             E.g. "TheScript:TheTaskName" instead of "TheTaskName"
    --scriptTaskHelp            <TASK_NAME>     no        Displays the help of the specified script task. For the <TASK_NAME> format, see --scriptTask.                                                  
    --listAvailableScriptTasks                  no        Lists all available script tasks from the SIP and the loaded project.
                                                             Note: The list will differ, if --project was specified, because project scripts will be loaded.
    --scriptLocations           <DIR_PATH>      no        Specifies an additional directory to search for scripts.
                                                             Separate multiple directories by a ','
                                                               Syntax: --scriptLocations "Directory1,Directory2 ..."
                                                             This option is also valid in the use cases UI and Code generation.
    --taskArgs                  <TASK_ARGS>     no        Passes arguments to the specified script tasks.
                                                             The arguments have the following syntax:
                                                               Syntax: --taskArgs "<TaskName>" "<Arguments to Task>"
                                                                  E.g. --taskArgs "MyTask" "-s --projectCfg MyFile.cfg" 
                                                             If only one task is executed, the "<TaskName>" can be omitted.
                                                             If multiple tasks are executed you have to specify the task name before the arguments.
                                                             The --scriptTaskHelp of a task will print the possible arguments to a script task
                                                             For multiple task arguments the following syntax apply:
                                                             Syntax: --taskArgs "<TaskName>" "<Arguments to Task>" "<TaskName2>" "<Arguments to Task2>"
                                                               E.g.: --taskArgs "MyTask" "-s --projectCfg MyFile.cfg" "Task2" "-d --saveTo saveFile.txt"
                                                             If the task name is not unique, your can specify the full qualified name with script name
                                                               E.g.  --taskArgs "MyScript:MyTask" "-s --projectCfg MyFile.cfg" 
                                                             Arguments with spaces inside the script task argument could be quoted with ""<Content>""
                                                               E.g.  --taskArgs "MyScript:MyTask" "-s --projectCfg ""Path to File\MyFile.cfg"" -d"
    --enableDebugger            <PORT>          no        Enables the Java debugger support for script task execution. The <PORT> argument specifies the Java debug TCP port.
                                                             The <PORT> argument is optional and the default value is 8000.
    --waitForDebugger                           no        Waits until a Java debugger is connected before executing any code                                                         


     At least one --scriptTask, --scriptTaskHelp or --listAvailableScriptTasks option must be specified.
     
     
Use case: Export
    Exports files from a given DaVinci project or AUTOSAR files into AUTOSAR files. 
    The content of the exported files is defined via the Exporter-ID.
    Various Exporter-IDs are available, e.g. for exporting one ECUC file per variant in a variant project.
    
    Usage examples:
    DVCfgCmd --project Project.dpa --exportDir ./exportDir --exportWithExporterId everything
    DVCfgCmd --project Project.dpa --exportDir ./exportDir --exportPostbuildVariants
    DVCfgCmd --project File.arxml  --exportDir ./exportDir --exportPreBuildVariants "MyVariantA"
    DVCfgCmd -p File1.arxml -p File2.arxml --exportDir ./exportDir --exportPreBuildVariants --exportWithExporterId everything
    
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option                     | Arguments    | Mandat. | Description                                       |
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -p, --project               <PROJECT_FILE>  yes       Specifies the absolute path to the DPA project file or AUTOSAR files to load.
                                                            You could also specify the option multiple times to load multiple AUTOSAR files into one project
                                                            E.g. -p File1.arxml -p File2.arxml
    --exportDir                 <DIR>           yes       Specifies the directory to export the data into.
                                                            Not required for --listAvailableExporterIds
    --exportPostbuildVariants                   no        Export the PostBuild variants info.
                                                            This will export the Active Ecuc and miscellaneous data
                                                              - Active Ecuc is exported into one file (even for split DPA-projects) per variant
                                                                <project-name>.<variant-name>.ecuc.arxml
                                                              - Miscellaneous data into one file per variant
                                                                <project-name>.<variant-name>.misc.arxml
                                                                The files contain all data of the project except:
                                                                    * ModuleConfigurations, ModuleDefinitions
                                                                    * BswImplementations, EcuConfigurations
                                                                    * Variant information like EvaluatedVariantSet
                                                            You can also combine this option with --exportWithExporterId to select another exporter 
                                                                E.g. --exportPostbuildVariants --exportWithExporterId everything will export the whole project.  
    --exportPreBuildVariants   <VARIANTS>       no        Exports all PreBuild variants.
                                                             This will export the Active Ecuc and miscellaneous data
                                                              - Active Ecuc is exported into one file (even for split DPA-projects) per variant
                                                                <project-name>.<variant-name>.ecuc.arxml
                                                              - Miscellaneous data into one file per variant
                                                                <project-name>.<variant-name>.misc.arxml
                                                                The files contain all data of the project except:
                                                                    * ModuleConfigurations, ModuleDefinitions
                                                                    * BswImplementations, EcuConfigurations
                                                             You can optionally specify a single variant to export by name
                                                               E.g. --exportPreBuildVariants VariantName
                                                             You can also combine this option with --exportWithExporterId to select another exporter.
                                                             This will export the data of the specified exporter for each PreBuild variant.
                                                               E.g. --exportPreBuildVariants --exportWithExporterId everything 
                                                                 This will export the whole AUTOSAR data per variant.  
    --listAvailableExporterIds                  no        Lists all available Exporter for the project,
                                                            which could be used for --exportWithExporterId <ID>
    --exportWithExporterId      <EXPORTER_IDS>  no        Exports the loaded project with the selected exporters.
                                                            This will create a file named Exported_<EXPORTER_ID>.arxml
                                                            in the folder specified by --exportDir.
                                                            Separate multiple exporterIds by a ','. 
                                                              Syntax: --exportWithExporterId "<exporterId>,<exporterId> ..."
                                                            An <EXPORTER_ID> could be retrieved with the --listAvailableExporterIds argument.
                                                            The id 'everything' will export all contained AUTOSAR data of all input files.
    --sort                      <ORDER>         no         Sorts the exported ARXML file(s) content according to the specified <ORDER>.
                                                             E.g. --sort "ecucAutosarOrder"
                                                               Supported <ORDER> argument(s):
                                                                  ecucAutosarOrder
                                                                     Sorts EcuC data (modules, containers and parameters) according to the ordering rules
                                                                     specified in the AUTOSAR 4 standard (see ECUConfiguration TPS [ecuc_sws_6066-69]).

                                                            
    The options --exportPostbuildVariants and --exportPreBuildVariants can be combined with --exportWithExporterId.
    In this case one file per variant named Exported_<EXPORTER_ID>.<variant-name>.arxml is being exported
    into the folder specified by --exportDir.
    
    At least one --export option or --listAvailableExporterIds must be specified.                                                                                                 
                                                                


Use case: Project update
	Run the project update for a given DaVinci project.

    Usage example:
    DVCfgCmd -u D:\TEMP\MyProj\MyProject.dpa
    DVCfgCmd -u D:\TEMP\MyProj\MyProject.dpa -e D:\TEMP\MyProj\extract1.arxml,D:\TEMP\MyProj\extract2.arxml --ecu myInstance
    
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option             | Arguments                            | Mandat. | Description                                                                                        |
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -u, --updateProject <DPA-FILE>                               yes     The DPA file of the project which shall be updated
    -e, --extract       <FILE>                                   no      The system extract file(s) or the legacy file(s) (DBC, Fibex, LDF). Separate multiple files by a ','
    --ecuInstance       <NAME>                                   depend  The Ecu instance name, mandatory for system description or legacy file with more than one Ecu instance. Not mandatory for system extract.  
    --psc               <FILE>                                   no      Path to the Project Standard Configuration file(s). Separate multiple files by a ','
    
    --odx               <ODX-FILE> <ECU> <VARIANT>               no      Path to ODX file, ODX ECU, ODX VARIANT	
    --csv               <CSV>                                    depend  Path to the State Description file
                                                                         If an ODX 2.0.1 Diagnostic Description file is used, an additional State description will be required.
    --did                                                        no      Import DIDs and RIDs as single signal. This settings is only relevant for ODX input files. 
			
    --cdd               <CDD-FILE> <ECU> <VARIANT>               no      Path to CDD file, CDD ECU, CDD VARIANT
    
    --patch             <PATCH-FILE>                             no      Diagnostic Description Patch File
      
    --onlyEcuc                                                   no      If no argument is passed, the complete update is performed (including DaVinci Developer workspace).
                                                                         If --onlyEcuc is set, only ECUC is updated, without the DaVinci Developer workspace.


    The options --cdd and --odx are mutual exclusive.            
   


Use case: Sign script
    Create a signature file for Python scripts to be executed during the DaVinci Configurator project update workflow.
    The signature file (extension .dvkey) will be created in the same folder as the script file.

    Usage example:
    DVCfgCmd --sign MyScript.py
    
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option                     | Arguments    | Mandat. | Description                                       |
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    --sign                      <SCRIPT_FILE>   yes       Specifies the script file to sign.



Use case: Common project operations
    Executes common project specific operations.

    Usage example:
    DVCfgCmd --project Project.dpa --reportFile "D:\report.html"
    
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option                     | Arguments    | Mandat. | Description                                       |
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -p, --project               <PROJECT_FILE>  yes       Specifies the absolute path to the DPA project file or the ECUC file.
   
    --reportFile                <REPORT_FILE>   yes        Specifies the path to the EcuConfigurationReport file to export the current ECU configuration to a HTML file. 
                                                             Syntax: --reportFile <REPORT_FILE>
                                                               E.g. --reportFile "D:\reports\DaVinciConfigurationReport.html"
                                                            With argument 'default' the report is exported to the default path (<PROJECT_PATH>\Log\DaVinciCfgReport.html)

    --reportArgs                <REPORT_ARGS>   no        Specifies special arguments for the EcuConfigurationReport.
                                                             Separate multiple arguments by a ','.
                                                               Syntax: --reportArgs "Argument1,Argument2 ..."
                                                                 E.g.  --reportArgs "IncludeAnnotations"
                                                             Available arguments:
                                                               * IncludeAnnotations
                                                               * IncludePlatformFunctions

Common parameters:
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   |Option                     | Arguments    | Mandat. | Description                                       |
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -h, --help                                  no       Shows this help.
    --verbose                   <Level>         no       Enables the verbose output of logging messages.
                                                            Possible arguments: ERROR, WARN, INFO
                                                            Optional Argument: The Log-Level for the verbose output. 
                                                            Default: ERROR
    -l, --logfile               <FILE>          no       Set the file to log the Console output into.
    --consoleEncoding           <CharsetName>   no       Set the encoding of the console output.
                                                            If no argument is passed, a list of all supported charsets is printed.
                                                            If no --consoleEncoding is set, the default charset UTF-8 is used.
                                                            You should set the encoding according to your commandline.
                                                            If you pass the charset name "consoleDefault",
                                                            the tool will try to use your currently default charset of
                                                            your commandline console. 
                                                                Note: All file outputs are always UTF-8 encoded.
                                                            
    

 
